h four uh 11 and 12.  now zooming into chromosome 11 they  discovered that  uh this is actually a nucleotide binding  lucerin rich repeat which plays a  crucial role in disease resistance  but they also noted that uh expression  of uh significant picks on chromosome 11  across environments were not stable uh  the authors of this paper later  concluded that cbsd is polygenic and  unstable across  environments so to expand on these  findings from this paper  my research aims to provide a  comprehensive study  to uncover the genetic architecture of  cbsd by complementing the  one to five scoring scale with improved  phenotyping methods like viral titer  and image analysis so there are four i  will be standardizing the triple  sandwich ant body elisa  to expand on its sensitivity for high  throughput tighter quantification  the question that you'd have is why  don't we use good standard methods like  the qpcr  these methods are expensive and many  studies have shown that  if elisa is carried out under optimal  conditions  it could produce results that are  equivalent to those of the qpcr  secondly we hope to undertake image  analysis to provide uh  image analysis for root necrosis to  provide quantitative measures of the  necrotic lesions across the cassava root  uh we also hope to identify markers  associated to cbsd through genome-wide  association studies  and in the end we hope to understand the  fresher gene expression  when it comes to cbsd using rna-seq  so the triple sun the triple sandwich  the triple antibody elisa uses three ant  bodies  and this method is sensitive specific  reproducible  and affordable now it's important that  we develop such methods  uh we standardized touch methods like  elisa to  enable us understand the genetic  mechanism of cbsd  but it's also crucial that we start  selecting for low viruta in our breeding  programs  so selecting for low varicata means that  we will reduce  disease pressure which in turn will  throw will  slow cbsd epidemics and to a smaller  extent reduce the  chances of viral evolution currently  we are working with the cycle two  population of the ugandan breeding  program  we are working with about 450 clones  these are being evaluated in two  environments uh we hope to collect  tighter data at three  six and nine months after planting to  ensure that we study  about viral progression throughout the  grow the growing period  then while at 12 months after planting  we will use the root  uh to determine virata  using qpcr and also fta cards  so to summarize uh the elisa methodology  for the for the for the leaves we are  using  pulled leaves since we are working with  at plot basis not plant bases  and then we conduct elisa acids on these  leaves  and from this we look at the data as a  semi-quantitative and quantitative  analysis  then we generate the title and at 12  months after planting  we take the root and then  carry out qpcr to identify various  the viral species and also the  tita and this is also done with the fta  cards  so we'll still have varitita for the  root using two methods  now in the incident that the analyzer we  don't trust the results  from analyzer plate we have stored extra  samples as herbariums  so we could conduct simple random  sampling on the leaves  and make sure that we carry out qpcr on  those leaves  to confirm the viral title so the reason  why we are robustly looking at varieta  is that uh studies have shown that the  virus replicates and accum  and accumulates differently in the  leaves and the roots  so if we have to understand the  mechanism of cbsd  viral title we need to look at both  tissues carefully  so from viral tita we will be looking at  uh  root necrosis using image analysis we're  using the phenol apps in collaboration  with mercurial university  now it's important to know that truth  necrosis is the most devastating symptom  of cbsd  it affects the root which is of economic  importance to us  but surprisingly it's the least studied  symptom  because it's only evaluated once in the  growing season  that is at harvest so we think that  understanding root necrosis using image  analysis  will enable us classify necrotic  patterns  uh it will provide also quantitative  measures like the number the size and  the location of necrotic lesions  across the cassava root we also hope to  understand relationships between these  quantitative image  best necrotic measurements uh viraltita  and the one to five scoring scale  so at harvest the root uh the root is  the harvested root is uh sliced and  images are taken at the proxima the  middle and the distal part  of the root so this is an example  of images taken from one experiment in  one location  and you can see there is a difference  with necrotic expression  when across the root so sometimes  necrosis happens as a large necrotic  patch  sometimes uh they are very small  necrotic patches  sometimes it starts at the center  sometimes it's set at the edge and other  times it's just  random so we think that to explore  necrosis we will have to generate data  about the percentage of necrosis across  the root  the size of the lesions the number of  the lesions and the distribution  of the lesions so just progress on both  eliza work  and the root necrosis work is we've acid  uh  both traits in two uh two  regions that have uh that vary in  disease pressure  and we're currently processing the data  so the next objective will be to use all  these new phenotyping methods to find  genome equations  associated with cbsd by conducting  genome-wide association studies  now i'll talk about the next thing will  be to talk about just the trait  heritabilities from the recent  experiments that we have vested  uh it's good news to note that uh  most heritabilities range from moderate  to high  which actually means that the  variability that we are observing is due  to genetic differences  but the exciting thing about this this  powerpoint this slide for me  is the semi-quantitative elisa essay  heritabilities and the quantitative  elisa rc heritabilities  this is the first time we are we're  using we are studying this chart and the  heritabilities are good to work with  now when we look at the correlations of  uh the cbs the traits generated by the  one to five  uh scale and the quantitative assay you  can see these ones in blue  that range from moderate to high  correlation  positive correlation are generated by  the one to five scoring scale  but when you look at the  semi-quantitative uh  title and the quantitative title there's  a zero correlation between  these two and the traits that  are asset using the one to five scale so  now  this confirms our need to broaden the  phenotyping methods when it comes to  cbsd  and include varieta as an independent  trait so i will end here and i would  like to acknowledge uh  my committee that's headed by john look  michelle heck  and keith perry i would also love to  acknowledge uh jean luc and max sarah's  lab  dmz in germany and the best part  is the team in namulong uganda who have  helped carry out  my work headed by dr robert i now  welcome  questions  i think there's a question from mike  gore in the chat  if you can see that or i can read it  please read it i don't seem to find the  chat yeah mike says  is it possible to sequence the viruses  from fta cards to id  viral strains and study viral population  diversity and evolution across cycles of  genomic selection  this would help investigate how genomic  selection in the plant population  shapes the virus population the answer  is yes i think it's possible  and we will test it since this is the  first time we're using the fta cards  okay um it looks like we don't have any  more questions  okay just one more one if anybody has  feedback please  send me an email and i'll answer you  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu

